// EXPECT: PASS
// FABLE5 2026-07-27. The keying discriminator for R1, and the reason it is worth its own fixture:
// bare-name-keyed maps are a FOUR-times-proven defect generator in this compiler
// (`method_contracts`, `fn_declared_effects`, `fn_effect_rows`, the resolver). A field called `k` is
// declared `secret<i64>` on `Vault` and plain `i64` on `Public`. Reading `p.k` must PASS.
// If R1's lookup ever regresses to keying on the bare field name, `Vault`'s declaration would
// contaminate `Public` and this fixture goes red — which is exactly the alarm we want, because the
// failure would be silent over-rejection on an unrelated type.
// The paired reject side is `declared_secret_struct_field_print_rejects`.
struct Vault { k: secret<i64> }
struct Public { k: i64 }
fn main() {
    let p = Public { k: 7 };
    print(p.k);
}
